@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Foundations-Typography a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Foundations: Typography";
    ds:summary "This document  covers the foundations of the type scale decisions foundational to the Canonical design language, including: \n\n- The mathematical foundation of the type scale (formula, parameters, full sequence)\n- Per-tier heading hierarchies – which sizes, weights, and line heights apply to h1–h6 and body text in applications, documentation, and editorial\n- Weight pairing rules and emphasis conventions\n- Line height selection and baseline grid alignment\n- Responsive scaling behaviour\n- Accessibility constraints on minimum sizes";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "**Status:** Draft  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Last updated:** 2026-04-25\n\n\n\n## Table of contents\n\n1. [Problem statement and scope](#1-problem-statement-and-scope)\n2. [The modular scale](#2-the-modular-scale)\n3. [Domain type scales](#3-domain-type-scales)\n4. [Weights](#4-weights)\n5. [Line height and the baseline grid](#5-line-height-and-the-baseline-grid)\n6. [Cross-domain principles](#6-cross-domain-principles)\n7. [Accessibility](#7-accessibility)\n8. [Responsive behaviour](#8-responsive-behaviour)\n9. [Invariants](#9-invariants)\n10. [Tooling](#10-tooling)\n\n\n\n## Glossary\n\nTable 54\n\n\n\n## 1. Problem statement and scope\n\n### 1.1 The problem\n\nCanonical’s product ecosystem spans three broad tiers:\n\n- **Applications** (MAAS, Landscape, Launchpad) – dense, tool-oriented interfaces where users work in extended sessions, often in small windows alongside other software.\n- **Documentation** (discourse sites, installation guides, API references) – deep, structured content where authors need semantic emphasis at every heading level.\n- **Editorial** ([ubuntu.com](http://ubuntu.com), [canonical.com](http://canonical.com), blog posts, campaign pages) – short-session content where scannability, visual hierarchy, and impact drive comprehension.\n\nEach tier demands a different balance between information density and visual contrast. Applications need the most compact scale possible. Documentation needs moderate contrast with strong emphasis support. Editorial needs generous contrast to guide rapid scanning. No single heading hierarchy optimally serves all three.\n\nWithout a shared mathematical foundation, these three hierarchies risk diverging into ad-hoc size choices. The result is inconsistency across products, difficulty maintaining design tokens, and no principled way to resolve conflicts when content moves between tiers.\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\nThis spec covers:\n\n- The mathematical foundation of the type scale (formula, parameters, full sequence)\n- Per-tier heading hierarchies – which sizes, weights, and line heights apply to h1–h6 and body text in applications, documentation, and editorial\n- Weight pairing rules and emphasis conventions\n- Line height selection and baseline grid alignment\n- Responsive scaling behaviour\n- Accessibility constraints on minimum sizes\n\nThis spec does not cover:\n\n- Typeface selection (see the typeface spec)\n- Vertical and horizontal spacing between elements (see the spacing spec)\n- Grid column structure and layout zones (see the grid spec)\n- CSS implementation details or token naming conventions (see the `docs-typescale` reference implementation)\n- OpenType feature usage, letter-spacing, or text decoration\n\n### 1.3 Goals\n\n- **Harmonic consistency:** all type sizes across all three tiers derive from a single modular scale, ensuring visual coherence even when content crosses tier boundaries.\n- **Density fit:** each tier selects the portion of the scale that matches its density requirements – compact for applications, moderate for documentation, generous for editorial.\n- **Emphasis without proliferation:** heading hierarchies rely on weight pairing to distinguish levels, minimising the number of distinct sizes needed.\n- **Baseline grid integration:** every type size maps to a line height that is a multiple of the 4px baseline unit (`bU`), producing consistent vertical rhythm.\n\n\n\n## 2. The modular scale\n\n### 2.1 Foundation\n\nOur type scale is based on Spencer Mortensen’s refinement of the classical modular scale described by Robert Bringhurst in *The Elements of Typographic Style*¹ – a system with centuries of proven application in both print and digital typography.\n\nMortensen replaces the classical scale’s finite sequence with a continuous formula that identifies and corrects several outliers in the traditional progression. Because his improvements deliver greater mathematical precision – a quality central to our brand – we adopt his sequence as the basis for all three domain scales.\n\n### 2.2 The formula\n\nThe scale is expressed as a fractional-step geometric progression:\n\n```\nf(i) = f(0) × pow(ratio, i / n)\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- `f(0)` is the base type size – 1rem (16px) for editorial, 0.875rem (14px) for documentation and applications\n- `ratio` defines the harmonic relationship – 2 for octave-based scales\n- `n` represents the number of steps per octave – 5 in Mortensen’s classic scale\n- `i` is the position in the sequence\n\nThe result is an infinite sequence of harmonically related sizes. Each domain selects a contiguous subset of this sequence for its heading hierarchy.\n\n### 2.3 The full sequence\n\nThe following table lists every size in the scale that our system uses, from 6px up to 96px. Sizes are rounded to whole pixels for implementation; rem values assume a 16px root.\n\nTable 55\n\n> **Note:** The sub-10px sizes (steps –5 through –2) exist in the mathematical sequence but are rarely used in UI. They appear in captions, legal text, and subscripts where applicable.\n\n\n\n## 3. Domain type scales\n\nWe work with content broadly categorised into three tiers – applications, documentation, and editorial. Each has distinct constraints and established practices that merit a separate heading hierarchy. The three tiers share the modular scale as a common foundation but optimise for different points on the density–contrast spectrum.\n\n### 3.1 Applications\n\nContext: maximum information density\n\nApplications support users through extended interaction sessions. Unlike websites – where interactions are often brief and familiarity is limited – applications assume expertise and must be optimised for sustained focus. User testing shows that applications are frequently run in small windows alongside other software, reinforcing the need for the highest possible information density.\n\nOn Ubuntu, default type sizes for UI and terminals fall between 11px and 13px. Aligning our application type sizes as closely as web accessibility standards allow reduces the cognitive effort required when users switch between tools.\n\nThe application type scale\n\nTo achieve maximum density, body text, h6, and h5 all share the root type size of 14px. H5 and h6 are distinguished from body text by bold and bold small caps rather than by size. The remaining heading levels (h4/h3, then h2/h1) use the minimum size increments needed for clear distinction. The result is the most compact scale of all three tiers, using only three distinct sizes:\n\nTable 56\n\n**Size discipline:** 3 distinct sizes (14, 18, 24px).\n\n### 3.2 Documentation\n\nContext: information density with semantic clarity\n\nDocumentation typography prioritises information density while maintaining the ability to create semantic emphasis. Technical authors need to distinguish between regular text, *emphasis*, and **strong emphasis** at every heading level. This requirement drives the weight pairing strategy and a slightly broader size range than applications.\n\nThe documentation type scale\n\nBody text is set at 14px. Bold at 14px is reserved for strong emphasis in body copy. Headings are set in pairs of lighter and bolder weights, skipping one step in the modular scale between pairs for clear distinction and consistent rhythm:\n\nTable 57\n\n**Size discipline:** 4 distinct sizes (14, 18, 24, 32px).\n\n### 3.3 Editorial\n\nContext: scannability, clarity, impact\n\nEditorial content – including marketing sites, blog posts, and promotional materials – serves users who are scanning, evaluating, and consuming information in short, focused sessions. Higher contrast and more generous spacing facilitate quick comprehension and help users rapidly locate the information they need. Editorial typography therefore applies the strongest contrasts in type size to establish a clear visual hierarchy.\n\nThe editorial type scale\n\nBody text is set at 16px – the browser default, providing optimal accessibility compliance. Headings use the widest range of sizes, with the largest (h1 at 42px) providing strong visual impact:\n\nTable 58\n\n**Size discipline:** 3 distinct sizes (16, 24, 42px), though up to 5 are available in the scale for display use cases (e.g. 84px at weight 200 for hero headlines).\n\n### 3.4 Comparison\n\nTable 59\n\n\n\n## 4. Weights\n\nOur system uses font weight as a primary hierarchy signal, reducing dependence on type size alone. This requires sufficient contrast between weight values to prevent visual confusion.\n\n### 4.1 Weight contrast minimum\n\nAdjacent weights in any pairing must differ by at least 200 units. This rule applies to:\n\n- Weight pairs within a heading level (e.g. h1 at 500, h2 at 300 – a difference of 200)\n- Emphasis within a heading (e.g. h1 at 500, emphasised word at 700 – a difference of 200)\n- Body text and strong emphasis (regular 400, bold 600 – a difference of 200)\n\nSkipping the intermediate value (e.g. going from 400 to 600, not 400 to 500) ensures clear visual distinction at every size.\n\n### 4.2 Body text\n\nBody text is set in regular weight (400). The next available weight at the same size is 600 (semi-bold), used for strong emphasis. Weight 500 is skipped at body size to maintain the 200-unit minimum contrast.\n\n### 4.3 Heading weight pairing\n\nHeadings follow three rules:\n\n1. **Weight decreases as size increases.** Larger headings use lighter weights. For example, 18px documentation h3 uses weight 600, while 24px editorial h3 uses weight 500.\n2. **Pairs of headings share a type size and are distinguished by weight.** H1 and h2 share a size; h3 and h4 share a size. This halves the number of distinct sizes needed.\n3. **Weight pairs differ by at least 200 units.** Within a pair, the bolder heading uses weight 500 or 600; the lighter uses 300 or 400.\n\n### 4.4 Emphasis in headings\n\nEmphasis within headings uses a weight increment of 200 from the heading’s own weight:\n\n- h1 (weight 500) → emphasised words: 700\n- h2 (weight 300) → emphasised words: 500\n\n> **Note:** emphasis should be used sparingly if at all. When used, it should highlight only key words, never the entire heading.\n\n### 4.5 Available weights\n\nTable 60\n\n\n\n## 5. Line height and the baseline grid\n\n### 5.1 Line height ratios\n\nLine height ratios follow a traditional pattern: most generous at body text size, then progressively tighter as heading sizes increase. We start at **1.5** for body text – a ratio that provides optimal readability for sustained reading while maintaining reasonable space efficiency.\n\nAt display sizes, ratios compress toward **1.1**, reflecting the typographic convention that large text needs proportionally less leading to maintain visual cohesion.\n\n### 5.2 Snapping to the baseline grid\n\nAll line heights must be a multiple of the 4px baseline unit (`bU`). We select the multiple of the baseline unit that yields a ratio appropriate to the heading’s position in the hierarchy. The following table lists the recommended line height at every size in the scale:\n\nTable 61\n\n> **Note:** all line heights in our system are multiples of the 4px baseline unit.\n\n### 5.3 Baseline alignment with nudge tokens\n\nCSS line-height alone does not guarantee that text baselines land on the grid. The vertical position of a glyph’s baseline within its line box depends on the font’s ascender/descender metrics, which vary by typeface, size, and rendering engine.\n\nNudge tokens solve this by adding a measured `padding-top` to each text element, shifting the first baseline onto the nearest grid line. Nudge values are:\n\n- **Font-specific** – calculated for Ubuntu Sans\n- **Size-specific** – each size/line-height pair has its own nudge\n- **Platform-measured** – extracted from Chromium at 1× device pixel ratio, snapped to the nearest 0.0625rem (1px)\n\nThe baseline compensation model (defined in the spacing spec §2.6.2) preserves the element’s total block contribution:\n\n- `padding-top = nudge`\n- `baseline-compensation = bU − nudge`\n- `margin-bottom = semantic-spacing + baseline-compensation`\n\nThis means nudge tokens shift the baseline without consuming any of the element’s semantic spacing.\n\n> **Scope:** the nudge technique applies only to the editorial and documentation tiers. The application tier is exempt – all nudge tokens are intentionally set to `0`. See the spacing spec §2.6.3 for rationale.\n\n```css\n/* Example: documentation h1 at 32px / 40px line height, bU = 4px */\nh1 {\n  font-size: 2rem;\n  line-height: 2.5rem;\n  font-weight: 500;\n  padding-top: 0.039rem;              /* nudge */\n  margin-bottom: calc(\n    var(--space-after-semantic-h1)     /* e.g. 1rem */\n    + var(--space-after-comp-h1)       /* bU − nudge */\n  );\n  margin-top: 0;\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## 6. Cross-domain principles\n\n### 6.1 Restraint in type size usage\n\nThe modular scale offers an infinite sequence of sizes. Applied without restraint, it can introduce inconsistency and visual clutter. We take inspiration from Massimo Vignelli², who often limited his typography to just two type sizes.\n\nOur type scales make full use of a typeface’s range of styles rather than depending on size alone. By alternating lighter and bolder weights at body and heading levels, we eliminate the need for at least three extra type sizes. We also incorporate the newly available small caps in Ubuntu Sans, reinforcing a disciplined and economical approach to font sizing.\n\n### 6.2 Navigating a type family\n\nThe way we navigate a font family to craft our type scales is inspired by Robert Bringhurst’s \"grammatical roadmap\"¹ – a systematic method for traversing the styles available in a type family (roman, italic, bold, small caps, etc.) to build a clear typographic hierarchy.\n\nWe make a small departure: we use bold small caps instead of bold full caps (as in Bringhurst’s original roadmap), since full caps can be perceived as typographic shouting. The tall x-height of Ubuntu Sans makes our small caps slightly larger than those in classical typefaces, helping establish a clear hierarchy without resorting to capitalisation.\n\n### 6.3 The baseline grid\n\nThe baseline grid is the backbone of our typography. It provides accountability and rigour rarely seen on the web, especially at the enterprise level. Every typographic element, spacing decision, border, and UI component in our design system aligns to it.\n\nThis is not merely an aesthetic choice – it is a systematic approach that ensures:\n\n- **Visual consistency:** all elements adhere to an invisible vertical rhythm, rather than floating arbitrarily.\n- **Scalable precision:** everything in our design system – from a simple button to complex components and patterns – aligns to the baseline grid. This alignment is especially visible across columns, reinforcing a sense of precision and rigour.\n- **Quality assurance:** any visual inconsistency becomes immediately apparent. We provide a baseline grid visualisation utility (in both Figma and CSS) to highlight drift from the grid.\n\n### 6.4 Font stacks\n\nAll tiers use Ubuntu Sans as the primary typeface, with a system font fallback chain for environments where Ubuntu Sans is unavailable:\n\nTable 62\n\n\n\n## 7. Accessibility\n\n### 7.1 Minimum size requirements\n\nAll base type sizes meet or exceed WCAG 2.1 guidelines for minimum readable size:\n\n- **Editorial:** 16px (1rem) base provides optimal accessibility compliance and matches the browser default.\n- **Documentation and applications:** 14px (0.875rem) base meets minimum readable size requirements while optimising for information density.\n\nNo heading or body text in any tier falls below 14px. Smaller sizes in the scale (10–12px) are reserved for captions, labels, and legal text where reduced size is contextually appropriate.\n\n### 7.2 Contrast standards\n\nType size affects the minimum contrast ratio required by WCAG 2.1:\n\n- Text below 18px (or below 14px bold) requires a **4.5:1** contrast ratio against its background (AA level).\n- Text at or above 18px (or at/above 14px bold) qualifies as “large text” and requires a **3:1** ratio (AA level).\n\nAll heading and body combinations in our system meet at least AA contrast requirements when rendered in the default palette.\n\n\n\n## 8. Responsive behaviour\n\n### 8.1 Root em scaling\n\nAll tiers include an optional root em scaling feature that proportionally scales all `rem`-based measurements by increasing the root font size on larger screens. The current default factor is **1.125** (18px root instead of 16px), applied above the large breakpoint (currently 1680px).\n\nBecause our type sizes, line heights, and spacing are all expressed in `rem`, this single change effectively “zooms” the entire UI without breaking any proportional relationships.\n\n### 8.2 Documentation and applications\n\nDocumentation and application tiers use consistent sizing across breakpoints. No size increases occur on larger screens beyond the optional root em scaling.\n\n### 8.3 Editorial\n\nIn the editorial domain, we scale larger headings (h1/h2 and h3/h4) on smaller screens to preserve presentation quality. Body text remains at 16px across all breakpoints for accessibility.\n\nEditorial domains implement root em scaling by default for larger screens.\n\n\n\n## 9. Invariants\n\nThe following rules are non-negotiable. Any implementation that violates them is considered a bug.\n\nTable 63\n\n### 9.1 Invariant priority\n\nWhen invariants conflict – for example, if an unusual size would satisfy baseline alignment but violate the single-scale rule – resolve in the order listed above. The single-scale rule takes precedence over all others.\n\n\n\n## 10. Tooling\n\nImplementing baseline grid alignment traditionally required manual measurement and adjustment – a tedious, error-prone process that limited adoption of baseline grids on the web. We contribute open-source tooling that automates this process.\n\n### 10.1 npm package\n\nThe [@lyubomir-popov/baseline-nudge-generator](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@lyubomir-popov/baseline-nudge-generator) package automates baseline alignment by:\n\n- Extracting font metrics from TTF/WOFF files programmatically\n- Calculating precise CSS nudge values required for baseline alignment\n- Generating design tokens that maintain mathematical relationships\n- Providing interactive demos with baseline grid overlays for verification\n\n### 10.2 Shell script\n\nThe [unified-type-scale.sh](https://github.com/lyubomir-popov/docs-typescale) script generates modular scale sequences:\n\n```bash\n# Continuous formula mode (Mortensen)\n./unified-type-scale.sh continuous <f0> <ratio> <n> [rounding]\n\n# Classic typographic scale mode (harmonic ratios)\n./unified-type-scale.sh classic <f0> <n> [rounding]\n```\n\nRounding options: `none` (4 decimal places), `floor`, `round`, `0.5` (default – snaps to nearest half-pixel).\n\n### 10.3 Figma plugin\n\nA Figma plugin reads the typography configuration and automatically generates text components with correct sizes, weights, line heights, and nudge values. This ensures design files stay synchronised with the token definitions.\n\n### 10.4 Spreadsheet reference\n\nA [spreadsheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1_zfQX8JtJ6c7SAIChu0t08CFya2szX3PFD-_rVLDqq4/edit?gid=0#gid=0) provides a quick designer reference, implementing the full modular scale with recommended line heights and per-domain mappings.\n\n\n\n## References\n\n1. Robert Bringhurst, *The Elements of Typographic Style*. The grammatical roadmap for navigating a type family appears on p. 55; the classical modular scale is discussed in chapters 8.3–8.4.\n2. Massimo Vignelli, *The Vignelli Canon*, p. 61 – on limiting typography to two type sizes. Available from the [Vignelli Center](https://www.rit.edu/vignellicenter/sites/rit.edu.vignellicenter/files/documents/The%20Vignelli%20Canon.pdf).\n3. Spencer Mortensen, [“Typographic Scale”](https://spencermortensen.com/articles/typographic-scale/) – the mathematical refinement of the classical scale adopted as our foundation.\n4. W3C, [WCAG 2.1 Success Criterion 1.4.3: Contrast (Minimum)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html).\n\n\n\n## Spec history\n\nTable 64";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
